-- What a top level will not accept - the other half of TopLevel.yana.
--
-- A module's top level runs in the order it is written, so the mistakes are the ones an order makes
-- possible: reading a name whose initializer has not run, and asking a module that has no such
-- sequence to run one anyway.
--
-- Only a *dynamically* initialized global can be read too early. A constant one holds its value
-- before the program starts and has no place in the sequence at all, so a statement above its
-- declaration reads exactly what it was declared as - which is why `later` and `pending` below are
-- written as calls rather than as numbers.

import Startup

data Point {x: Int, y: Int}

fn compute() -> Int = 7

-- Read before its own declaration. The name is in scope from the first line of the module - that is
-- what makes a global a global - so this resolves to something that exists and holds nothing yet.
let &early = later + 1
let &later = compute()

-- The same mistake through an assignment rather than a read, which is a different path into the
-- resolver and used to be the one with no check on it at all.
pending = 3
let &pending = compute()

-- Its own initializer, which is why the check happens *after* the initializer is resolved rather
-- than before it: nothing about `self` is initialized until this statement finishes.
let &self = self + 1

-- A borrow has an extent and a global does not. What would be stored is the address of storage in
-- the entry function's own frame, which stops existing the moment the program starts.
fn xOf(return p: Point) -> 'Int = p.x

let &point = Point {x: 1, y: 2}
let &edge = xOf(point)

-- A module-level `let` has no `in`: what follows it is the rest of the file, which is already the
-- scope of the name it declares.
let scoped = 3 in scoped * 2

-- The one thing here that is a *warning* rather than a rejection, and it is one deliberately. A
-- global lives for the whole program and is never torn down - drop points are last-use-based, and a
-- global has no last use - so a program-lifetime resource is a real thing to want and the meaning of
-- this declaration is defined. What is not fine is writing an effect down and having it silently not
-- run, which is what the warning names.
data Handle {id: Int}

instance Drop(Handle):
    fn drop(->value: Handle) -> {}:
        pending = pending + value.id

let &held = Handle {id: 4}

-- And the entry point takes no arguments. There is no argument or environment model to fill them
-- from, so a `main` that declares one cannot be called by anything.
fn main(n: Int) -> Int = n + later
